{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "prop:set-inversion",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "As a direct application corollary of RenTelschowSchwartzman2024 to the simultaneous tube event, for every \\(k\\in\\mathbb R\\) at once, \\(\\mathcal C(k)\\subseteq\\widehat{\\mathcal C}_n(k)\\) and \\(\\{W(\\theta_c):c\\in\\mathcal C(k)\\}\\subseteq\\widehat{\\mathcal V}_n(k)\\). The inclusions require neither injectivity nor a nonzero derivative of the displayed path.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(k\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal C(k)\\)",
      "\\(\\widehat{\\mathcal C}_n(k)\\)",
      "\\(W\\)",
      "\\(\\theta_c\\)",
      "\\(c\\)",
      "\\(\\widehat{\\mathcal V}_n(k)\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:frontier-tube",
      "def:set-inversion",
      "thm:postselection-tube"
    ],
    "justification": "The eventwise union construction retains every compatible inverse branch without derivatives or local inversion.",
    "gap": "This is a direct reporting corollary, not a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "It returns all welfare values compatible with a chosen divergence level.",
    "proof_tex": "Define the simultaneous tube event\n\\[\n \\mathcal E_n^{\\mathrm{tube}}\n =\\left\\{(c,W(\\theta_c),K(\\theta_c))\\in\\widehat{\\mathcal T}_n\n          \\text{ for every }c\\in\\mathcal C\\right\\}.\n\\]\nBy \\(\\textnormal{thm:postselection-tube}\\),\n\\[\n \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}P(\\mathcal E_n^{\\mathrm{tube}})\\ge 1-\\alpha.\n\\]\nIt remains to prove the two asserted inclusions deterministically on the single\nevent \\(\\mathcal E_n^{\\mathrm{tube}}\\).  Fix a sample point in that event, an\narbitrary \\(k\\in\\mathbb R\\), and an arbitrary \\(c\\in\\mathcal C(k)\\).  The\ndefinition of \\(\\mathcal C(k)\\) gives\n\\[\n K(\\theta_c)=k.\n\\]\nThe divergence-coordinate inequality in the definition of\n\\(\\widehat{\\mathcal T}_n\\), evaluated at this same \\(c\\), gives\n\\[\n \\left|\\widehat K(\\widehat\\theta_c)-K(\\theta_c)\\right|\n \\le\n \\frac{s_K(c)(\\widehat q_{1-\\alpha}+\\eta)}{\\sqrt n}.\n\\]\nSubstitution of \\(K(\\theta_c)=k\\) yields\n\\[\n \\left|\\widehat K(\\widehat\\theta_c)-k\\right|\n \\le\n \\frac{s_K(c)(\\widehat q_{1-\\alpha}+\\eta)}{\\sqrt n}.\n\\]\nBy the definition of \\(\\widehat{\\mathcal C}_n(k)\\), this is exactly the\ncondition \\(c\\in\\widehat{\\mathcal C}_n(k)\\).  Since the chosen\n\\(c\\in\\mathcal C(k)\\) was arbitrary,\n\\[\n \\mathcal C(k)\\subseteq\\widehat{\\mathcal C}_n(k).\n\\]\n\nThe welfare-coordinate inequality defining \\(\\mathcal E_n^{\\mathrm{tube}}\\)\nat the same \\(c\\) is\n\\[\n \\left|\\widehat W(\\widehat\\theta_c)-W(\\theta_c)\\right|\n \\le\n \\frac{s_W(c)(\\widehat q_{1-\\alpha}+\\eta)}{\\sqrt n}.\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\n W(\\theta_c)\\in\n \\left[\n \\widehat W(\\widehat\\theta_c)\n   -\\frac{s_W(c)(\\widehat q_{1-\\alpha}+\\eta)}{\\sqrt n},\n \\widehat W(\\widehat\\theta_c)\n   +\\frac{s_W(c)(\\widehat q_{1-\\alpha}+\\eta)}{\\sqrt n}\n \\right].\n\\]\nBecause \\(c\\in\\widehat{\\mathcal C}_n(k)\\), this interval is one of the\nintervals in the union defining \\(\\widehat{\\mathcal V}_n(k)\\).  Hence\n\\[\n W(\\theta_c)\\in\\widehat{\\mathcal V}_n(k).\n\\]\nTaking all \\(c\\in\\mathcal C(k)\\) proves\n\\[\n \\{W(\\theta_c):c\\in\\mathcal C(k)\\}\n \\subseteq\\widehat{\\mathcal V}_n(k).\n\\]\n\nThe argument fixed only one event, \\(\\mathcal E_n^{\\mathrm{tube}}\\), and the\nchoice of \\(k\\) within that event was arbitrary.  Therefore both inclusions\nhold for every \\(k\\in\\mathbb R\\) simultaneously on\n\\(\\mathcal E_n^{\\mathrm{tube}}\\); there is no uncountable intersection of\nprobability-one events.  If \\(\\mathcal C(k)=\\varnothing\\), both inclusions are\nvacuous.  Otherwise the construction retains every element of\n\\(\\mathcal C(k)\\), including multiple inverse branches.  The proof uses only\nset membership and the two simultaneous tube inequalities: it neither assumes\ninjectivity nor differentiates the path or divides by \\(K'(c)\\).  Thus the\nsame construction remains valid for constant paths, tangencies,\nself-intersections, and failed-slope cases.  Combining the eventwise result\nwith the displayed lower bound for \\(P(\\mathcal E_n^{\\mathrm{tube}})\\)\ncompletes the proof.",
